ay T9, Play T3, Chi W5, Play W2, Play J3, Peng J1, Play T5, Play T7, Chi B6, Play T6, Play T6.\nThe followings are your valid actions:[Peng T4; Pass]\u3002", "reasoning": "", "output": "**Evaluation:**  \n\nFrom the game state:  \n- You have 5 hand tiles: W5, W6, T4\u00d72, B1, B3\u00d72.  \n- You have 2 melds already: [B2, B3, B4] (a Chow) and [T1, T2, T3] (a Chow).  \n- Next player discarded T4, so you can choose **Peng T4** or **Pass**.  \n- Prevailing wind is West (F3), seat wind is East (F1).  \n\nCurrently you have 7 tiles if you count melds:  \nHand: W5, W6, T4\u00d72, B1, B3\u00d72 \u2192 7 hand tiles + 6 melded tiles = 13 tiles; after Peng you'd replace a T4\u00d73 meld with your extra tile, so you would still need to form a complete hand of 4 melds + 1 pair (14 tiles total).  \n\nLet\u2019s see your hand shape:  \nAfter **Peng T4**:  \n- You would meld T4\u00d73, removing two T4 from hand.  \n- Remaining hand tiles: W5, W6, B1, B3\u00d72 (5 tiles, but one B3 is already used in your B2B3B4 meld? No \u2014 the B3 in hand are separate from the meld B2B3B4; B2B3B4 is one meld using one B3 from earlier; you still have two spare B3 in hand now).  \n\nActually, let's recount total tiles before Peng:  \n\nYour known tiles (hand + melds):  \nHand: W5, W6, T4, T4, B1, B3, B3  \nMelds: B2B3B4 (uses 1 B3)  \n       T1T2T3  \n\nTotal distinct physical tiles:  \nHand: 7  \nMelds: 6  \nTotal: 13 tiles (normal before drawing/discarding).  \n\nAfter Peng:  \nMelds: B2B3B4, T1T2T3, T4T4T4 (using your two T4 plus discarded T4)  \nHand left: W5, W6, B1, B3, B3 \u2192 5 tiles.  \n\nNeed to go to 4 melds + 1 pair. You currently have 3 melds + 5 hand tiles \u2192 need to form 1 more meld and a pair from the 5 hand tiles.  \n\nPossible pairs from W5, W6, B1, B3, B3: pair B3 (if keep both B3), or try to pair W5/W6/B1 with new draws.  \n\n**Point potential**:  \n- Peng T4 would give \"Pung of Terminals or Honors\" (2 pts) because T4 is simple tile, not terminal/honor? Wait T4 is simple, not 1,9, honor \u2014 so no Pung of Terminals or Honors.  \n- Your hand currently has B2B3B4 and T1T2T3 chows \u2014 both terminal chows? T1T2T3 is a terminal chow (T1 terminal), B2B3B4 not terminal chow. Might aim for Two Terminal Chows (1 pt) if we get another 123 or 789 chow? But T1T2T3 is already one terminal chow.  \n- No honors in hand so maybe \"No Honors\" (1 pt) later.  \n- All Chows (2 pts) possible if you make all 4 melds chows + a pair. You already have 2 chows, T4 pung breaks that unless you make last meld a chow.  \n\nBetter to **Pass** because:  \n1. Peng T4 breaks \"All Chows\" possibility (melds would have 2 chows + 1 pung).  \n2. You have two B3 in hand; possible to make B1B2B3 chow if you get B2, but B2 is in your meld already, so can't reuse tile in another meld.  \n3. Your hand shape flexible with T4 pair already \u2014 keeping T4 pair might allow going for \"Seven Pairs\" if you draw more pairs, or \"All Chows\" if you keep all melds as chows.  \n4. Peng now exposes tiles, limits concealed hand bonuses.  \n5. Tile efficiency: T4 is safe to discard later if needed, but opponents\u2019 discards show some T4 already out (you hold 2, next player discards 1, maybe few left).  \n\nGiven you need at least 8 pts to win, All Chows (2) + Two Terminal Chows (1) + No Honors (1) + maybe Self-drawn (1) etc. doesn\u2019t reach 8 easily, but Peng T4 doesn\u2019t add significant fan either. Might as well keep hand flexible and concealed.  \n\n**Final reason**: Passing keeps your hand concealed and retains options for All Chows, Seven Pairs, or other patterns without adding a pung that gives little value.  \n\n**Answer:** Pass"}
